         path_tier='core',
         description="You have stones with given weights. Repeatedly pick the two heaviest stones and smash them together: if they're equal weight, both are destroyed; if not, the lighter is destroyed and the heavier becomes (heavy - light). Return the weight of the last stone left, or 0 if none remain.",
         constraints='1 <= len(stones) <= 30; 1 <= stones[i] <= 1000.',
         function_signature='def last_stone_weight(stones):',
         starter_code="import heapq\n\ndef last_stone_weight(stones):\n    # You always need the two CURRENT largest stones. A max-heap gives\n    # you the largest in O(log n) -- Python's heapq is a min-heap, so\n    # push negated values to simulate one.\n    pass\n",
         expected_time_complexity='O(n log n)',
         expected_space_complexity='O(n)',
         brute_force_approach='Sort the stones after every smash to find the two heaviest -- O(n^2 log n) across all smashes.',
         optimal_approach='A max-heap (simulated in Python via negated values in a min-heap): repeatedly pop the two largest, smash them, and push the remainder back if nonzero.',
         common_mistakes="Forgetting to negate values both going INTO and coming back OUT of the heap (heapq is always a min-heap in Python -- there's no built-in max-heap); pushing 0 back onto the heap when two equal stones fully destroy each other (wastes a slot but isn't wrong; the real bug is forgetting to push the remainder at all when it's nonzero).",
         edge_cases="Single stone (that's the answer, nothing to smash); all stones equal (multiple full-destroy pairs, possibly one leftover if odd count); two stones only.",
         test_inputs=[([2, 7, 4, 1, 8, 1],), ([1],), ([2, 2],), ([1, 3],)],
         reference_solution='import heapq\n\ndef last_stone_weight(stones):\n    heap = [-s for s in stones]\n    heapq.heapify(heap)\n    while len(heap) > 1:\n        first = -heapq.heappop(heap)\n        second = -heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if first != second:\n            heapq.heappush(heap, -(first - second))\n    return -heap[0] if heap else 0\n',
         hints=["You repeatedly need 'the two biggest stones right now' -- a heap gives you fast access to an extreme value, but Python's heapq only gives you the SMALLEST directly.", "Simulate a max-heap by pushing the negation of every stone's weight. The smallest negated value corresponds to the largest actual weight. Remember to negate back when you pop.", 'heap = [-s for s in stones]; heapify. while len(heap) > 1: first = -heappop(heap); second = -heappop(heap); if first != second: heappush(heap, -(first - second)). Return -heap[0] if heap else 0.']
