         function_signature='def can_finish(num_courses, prerequisites):',
         starter_code="def can_finish(num_courses, prerequisites):\n    # Build an adjacency list, then DFS from each course. A course can't\n    # be finished if following its prerequisites ever leads back to a\n    # course you're CURRENTLY in the middle of visiting -- that's a cycle.\n    pass\n",
         expected_time_complexity='O(V + E)',
         expected_space_complexity='O(V + E)',
         brute_force_approach="There isn't a simpler brute force -- detecting whether ANY cycle exists in a directed graph fundamentally requires exploring the graph's structure.",
         optimal_approach="DFS with 3-state tracking per course (unvisited / currently-being-visited / fully-done): if DFS ever reaches a course that's currently-being-visited (not just previously visited), that's a cycle -- courses can never be finished.",
         common_mistakes="Using only a simple visited/unvisited boolean (2 states) instead of 3 states -- this fails to distinguish 'I'm still in the middle of exploring this course's prerequisites' from 'I already fully finished exploring this course', and will report false cycles or miss real ones; forgetting to mark a course as fully-done after its DFS completes.",
