         optimal_approach="A min-heap holding the current front node of every still-nonempty list (tagged with a tie-breaking index, since Node objects aren't directly orderable). Repeatedly pop the smallest, append it to the result, and push that list's next node (if any) back onto the heap.",
         common_mistakes="Pushing raw Node objects onto the heap without a tie-breaker for equal values -- Python's heapq compares tuple elements left to right, and once two values tie it would try to compare the Node objects themselves, which crashes since Node doesn't define an ordering; forgetting to push the popped node's `.next` back onto the heap when it exists, silently dropping the rest of that list.",
